<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Sao</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Encountering Sorrow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Li Sao (Encountering Sorrow) is a long poem of about 370 lines attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qu-yuan">Qu Yuan</a>, minister of the State of Chu in the late fourth century BCE, and the opening work of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chu-ci">Chu Ci</a> anthology. In it a loyal noble slandered at court laments his fate and makes a visionary journey through the heavens in search of a worthy ruler. It is the longest poem of pre-imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and a model for later Chinese poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-sao.jpg" alt="Li Sao" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: White whirlwind · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_sao_illustr%C3%A9_(crop).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
